   "t": 65.5,
   "fen": "r1bqk2r/pp1pppbp/2n2np1/8/2BNP3/2N1B3/PPP2PPP/R2QK2R b KQkq - 5 7",
   "line": [
    "Bc4"
   ],
   "spoken": "White develops the bishop to c4, aiming at f7 — the sharp Sozin-style setup. Black just keeps developing; depending on White's next move it will be castling, or d6, or sometimes d5 in one go.",
   "kind": "main",
   "teaches": "Against the aggressive bishop on c4, complete development and keep the central break flexible.",
   "plans": "Castle, hold the d-pawn, and choose the break later."
  },
  {
   "ply": 14,
   "t": 71,
   "fen": "r1bq1rk1/pp1pppbp/2n2np1/8/2BNP3/2N1B3/PPP2PPP/R2QK2R w KQ - 6 8",
   "line": [
    "O-O"
   ],
   "spoken": "Castling first, and deliberately NOT the pawn to d6 yet — holding it back preserves maximum flexibility. That reserve pays off if White castles, when the one-move d5 break becomes available.",
   "kind": "main",
   "teaches": "Delaying a committal pawn move keeps your best break in reserve.",
   "plans": "Keep the d-pawn home until d5 in one move is on."
  },
  {
   "ply": 15,
   "t": 83,
   "fen": "r1bq1rk1/pp1pppbp/2n2np1/8/2B1P3/2N1B3/PPP1NPPP/R2QK2R b KQ - 7 8",
   "line": [
    "Nde2"
   ],
   "spoken": "The knight retreat to e2 is a poor choice — it steps off the strong d4-square passively and hands Black an immediate way to challenge in the centre.",
   "kind": "main",
   "teaches": "A passive retreat that abandons a strong central post invites an immediate counter.",
   "plans": "Hit back at once while White is disorganised."
  },
  {
   "ply": 16,
   "t": 126.5,
   "fen": "r1bq1rk1/pp1pppbp/2n3p1/8/2B1P1n1/2N1B3/PPP1NPPP/R2QK2R w KQ - 8 9",
   "line": [
    "Ng4"
   ],
   "spoken": "The knight leaps to g4, harassing the e3-bishop — a move that can be genuinely nasty here, since the bishop has no comfortable square that keeps everything defended.",
   "kind": "main",
   "teaches": "With the queen still home, the g4-knight jab against the e3-bishop is a standard Dragon resource.",
   "plans": "Follow up with the queen sortie to b6."
  },
  {
   "ply": 17,
   "t": 150,
   "fen": "r1bq1rk1/pp1pppbp/2n3p1/6B1/2B1P1n1/2N5/PPP1NPPP/R2QK2R b KQ - 9 9",
   "line": [
    "Bg5"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 18,
   "t": 151.5,
   "fen": "r1b2rk1/pp1pppbp/1qn3p1/6B1/2B1P1n1/2N5/PPP1NPPP/R2QK2R w KQ - 10 10",
   "line": [
    "Qb6"
   ],
   "spoken": "The queen swings to b6 — a garden-variety double attack, hitting the f2- and b2-pawns at once. It comes with risk, since the light-squared bishop is still at home, but the fork is real and White can only defend one pawn.",
   "kind": "main",
   "teaches": "A queen on b6 forking f2 and b2 is a classic Sicilian double attack — one pawn must fall.",
   "plans": "Take the pawn White doesn't defend."
  },
  {
   "ply": 19,
   "t": 174,
   "fen": "r1b2rk1/pp1pppbp/1qn3p1/6B1/2B1P1n1/2N5/PPP1NPPP/R2Q1RK1 b - - 11 10",
   "line": [
    "O-O"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 20,
   "t": 177,
   "fen": "r1b2rk1/pp1pppbp/2n3p1/6B1/2B1P1n1/2N5/PqP1NPPP/R2Q1RK1 w - - 0 11",
   "line": [
    "Qxb2"
   ],
   "spoken": "Grab it — the pawn on b2. The queen looks adventurous but it won't be trapped: the a3-square is a ready retreat and there are escape routes to spare, so this is simply a clean extra pawn.",
   "kind": "main",
   "teaches": "Before a deep pawn-grab, check the queen's escapes; with a3 available on b2 there is no trap.",
   "plans": "Retreat to a3 and consolidate the extra pawn."
  },
  {
   "ply": 22,
   "t": 189,
   "fen": "r1b2rk1/pp1pppbp/2n3p1/6B1/2B1P1n1/q1N5/P1P1NPPP/1R1Q1RK1 w - - 2 12",
   "line": [
    "Rb1",
    "Qa3"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 23,
   "t": 228.5,
   "fen": "r1b2rk1/pp1pppbp/2n3p1/3N2B1/2B1P1n1/q7/P1P1NPPP/1R1Q1RK1 b - - 3 12",
   "line": [
    "Nd5"
